BATCH={'key':'bosvid_wklo','label':'Boston, Massachusetts: halls, hotels, theatres and churches named in a walk through old Boston','added':'2026-11-27 02:33'}
PLACES=[
N('old-city-hall-boston','Old City Hall, Boston','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.35811,-71.05933,'exact','view','walk','','1862–1865',
  'Boston\'s city hall from 1865 to 1969, at 45 School Street on the Freedom Trail, designed by Gridley J. F. Bryant and Arthur Gilman. It was one of the first buildings in the French Second Empire style in the United States, and the style then spread to city halls from Providence to Philadelphia. Thirty-eight mayors served here; when the city moved out in 1969 the building was turned into offices and a restaurant, an early example of reuse of an old public building.',
  'The Second Empire front on School Street, between the Old South Meeting House and King\'s Chapel. The Boston Latin School stood on this site from 1704 to 1748. Inside are offices and a steakhouse; interior not checked.',
  accn='Privately managed office building (Wikipedia names the nonprofit Architectural Heritage Foundation as its developer). Seen from School Street, a public street on the Freedom Trail. The building\'s own site refused to open, so whether the lobby may be visited was not checked.',
  links=[W('Old City Hall (Boston)')],brick='?'),
N('custom-house-tower-boston','Custom House and Custom House Tower','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.35907,-71.05337,'exact','view','walk','','1837–1847; tower 1913–1915',
  'A Greek Revival custom house of 1837 to 1847 by Ammi Burnham Young, cross-shaped and domed, with 36 fluted Doric columns each cut from a single piece of Quincy granite. In 1913 to 1915 Peabody and Stearns built a 496-foot tower on top of it. Since 1997 it has been a Marriott timeshare hotel.',
  'The granite columns of the old custom house at street level, the tower rising out of its roof, and the 22-foot clock near the top. Wikipedia says the ground floor has a rotunda with maritime exhibits open to the public and the tower an observation deck on the 26th floor; whether and when visitors may go up was not checked.',
  accn='A Marriott hotel in McKinley Square, in the Custom House District (National Register, 1973). Seen from the streets round McKinley Square; the hotel\'s own pages were not read, so access inside is not stated.',
  links=[W('Custom House Tower')],brick='?'),
N('trinity-church-boston','Trinity Church, Copley Square','Boston','bos','worship','standing',42.35,-71.07556,'exact','open','walk','','1872–1877',
  'The Episcopal parish founded in 1733 lost its Summer Street church in the Great Boston Fire of 1872 and built this one on Copley Square, designed by Henry Hobson Richardson and consecrated in 1877 under the rector Phillips Brooks. It made Richardson\'s name and gave the country the heavy round-arched style called Richardsonian Romanesque.',
  'The church, built of Dedham granite, seen across Copley Square. The church is open for paid tours and holds services every Sunday and on weekdays; Friday organ recitals at 12:15 p.m. from September to May (its tours page).',
  accn='Trinity Church in the City of Boston (Episcopal), on Copley Square. Admission for touring; tours pause on Tuesdays from noon to 12:45 for the Eucharist, and there are no tours on Sundays and Mondays (tours page, read November 2026).',
  links=[W('Trinity Church (Boston)'),['Trinity Church: tours','https://www.trinitychurchboston.org/visit/tours'],['Trinity Church: worship','https://www.trinitychurchboston.org/worship']],
  brick='no',hours='Tours Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. (from January Wednesday to Saturday); Sunday service 10 a.m. September to June, 9 a.m. and 5 p.m. in summer'),
N('boston-public-library-mckim-building','Boston Public Library, McKim Building','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.34944,-71.07806,'exact','open','walk','','1888–1895',
  'The central library of the Boston Public Library, on the west side of Copley Square, designed by Charles Follen McKim in the Renaissance Revival and Beaux-Arts styles. Work began in 1888 and the building opened in February 1895, though its painted and carved art took two more decades to finish. It holds the library\'s research collection; a later addition behind it is a separate building.',
  'The long arcaded front on Copley Square, the stair hall, the reading rooms and the murals inside, and the arcaded courtyard. The public may walk in during opening hours.',
  accn='Boston Public Library, on Copley Square. Free. Hours from the library\'s own page for the Central Library, read November 2026.',
  links=[W('Boston Central Library'),['BPL: Central Library hours','https://www.bpl.org/locations/central/']],
  brick='?',hours='Monday to Thursday 9 a.m. to 8 p.m.; Friday and Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('quincy-market-boston','Quincy Market (Faneuil Hall Market)','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.36,-71.055,'site','open','walk','','1824–1826',
  'Three market buildings beside Faneuil Hall, designed by Alexander Parris and opened on August 26, 1826, after Mayor Josiah Quincy pushed to replace the old market. The domed central building was put up by the city in granite; the plainer brick-and-granite North and South Markets were built by the merchants. After decline and talk of demolition in the mid-20th century they were restored and reopened as a festival marketplace in 1976.',
  'The central building\'s granite porticoes and dome, with food stalls inside, and the North and South Markets on either side, whose fronts mix brick with granite. A National Historic Landmark. The streets round it are paved in granite and Belgian blocks.',
  accn='Faneuil Hall Marketplace (privately run), 4 South Market Street. Open ground on all sides; the central hall is a food market. The marketplace\'s own page gives street performers daily 11 a.m. to 9 p.m. and the 2026 holiday market 11 a.m. to 7 p.m., but no general opening hours.',
  links=[W('Quincy Market'),['Faneuil Hall Marketplace','https://faneuilhallmarketplace.com/']],
  brick='some',hours='?'),
N('south-station-headhouse-boston','South Station Headhouse','Boston','bos','rail','standing',42.35194,-71.05528,'exact','open','walk','','1899',
  'Boston\'s union station for the railroads running south and west, built in 1899 to replace four terminals, designed by Shepley, Rutan and Coolidge in the Classical Revival style. As rail traffic fell the bankrupt railroads sold it in 1965 and part was torn down in 1975; plans to pull down the rest fell through and the headhouse went on the National Register as South Station Headhouse. It is still the busiest railroad station in Greater Boston.',
  'The curved granite front on Dewey Square with its unfluted Ionic columns. Inside is the working concourse for Amtrak and commuter trains.',
  accn='Massachusetts Bay Transportation Authority, 700 Atlantic Avenue. A working station: the concourse is open to travellers; its opening hours were not checked. Red Line and Silver Line subway stations underneath.',
  links=[W('South Station')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('horticultural-hall-boston','Horticultural Hall','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.3431,-71.08511,'exact','view','walk','','1901',
  'The third hall of the Massachusetts Horticultural Society, built in 1901 at Huntington and Massachusetts Avenues in the English Renaissance Revival style by Wheelwright and Haven, the firm that also drew the Harvard Lampoon building. Its ten-day opening filled the hall with flowers, among them a thousand orchids, and for decades it housed garden clubs and plant societies. Northeastern University has owned it since 2020.',
  'The front facing Symphony Hall across Massachusetts Avenue. Inside are offices and a library; interior not checked.',
  accn='Owned by Northeastern University; offices. Seen from Huntington and Massachusetts Avenues.',
  links=[W('Horticultural Hall (Boston)')],brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('cyclorama-building-boston','Cyclorama Building','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.34470,-71.07120,'exact','view','walk','','1884',
  'A round building of 1884 in the South End, put up to show a 400-by-50-foot painting of the Battle of Gettysburg, with a steel-trussed dome 127 feet across, then second in size in the country only to the dome of the Capitol. When the fashion for cycloramas passed it held roller skating, boxing and other shows, then from 1923 to 1970 the Boston Flower Exchange. It is now run by the Boston Center for the Arts.',
  'The domed hall at 543-547 Tremont Street, used for events and exhibitions. On the National Register since 1973. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Boston Center for the Arts, 543-547 Tremont Street. Seen from Tremont Street; the arts centre\'s page would not open, so when the hall may be visited was not checked.',
  links=[W('Cyclorama Building (Boston)')],brick='?'),
N('hotel-vendome-boston','Hotel Vendome','Boston','bos','hotel','standing',42.35126,-71.07864,'exact','view','walk','burned','1871; enlarged 1881',
  'A luxury hotel of 1871 at Commonwealth Avenue and Dartmouth Street in Back Bay, enlarged in 1881. It was being turned into flats when fire broke out on June 17, 1972; after the fire was under control a corner section of all five floors collapsed and killed nine firefighters, the worst single loss in the history of the Boston Fire Department. It reopened in 1975 as condominiums and shops.',
  'The old hotel on Commonwealth Avenue. On the Commonwealth Avenue Mall, a few yards from the site of the fire, a memorial to the nine firefighters: a bronze helmet and coat laid over a low arc of dark granite.',
  accn='Private condominiums and businesses. Seen from Commonwealth Avenue and Dartmouth Street; the memorial is on the public mall.',
  links=[W('Hotel Vendome fire')],brick='?'),
N('mother-church-christian-science-boston','The First Church of Christ, Scientist (the Mother Church)','Boston','bos','worship','standing',42.34443,-71.08487,'exact','open','walk','','1894; extension 1904–1906',
  'The mother church of Christian Science. The first church, a Richardsonian Romanesque building in New Hampshire granite with a 126-foot steeple and an octagonal auditorium seating 900, was finished in December 1894. The much larger domed Mother Church Extension was added in 1904 to 1906 to designs begun by Charles Brigham.',
  'The 1894 church with its steeple, dwarfed by the domed extension beside it; the extension holds one of the largest pipe organs in the world, built in 1952. The plaza round them was laid out in the 1960s.',
  accn='The First Church of Christ, Scientist. Its own page says the church and the plaza are open to all, with Sunday services, Wednesday testimony meetings, a public reading room and free tours that begin in the Welcome Hall; the times were not given on the page read.',
  links=[W('The First Church of Christ, Scientist'),['The Mother Church','https://www.christianscience.com/the-mother-church']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('cutler-majestic-theatre-boston','Cutler Majestic Theatre','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.3516,-71.0651,'site','open','walk','','1903',
  'A Beaux-Arts theatre of 1903 on Tremont Street, designed by John Galen Howard for Eben Dyer Jordan of the Jordan Marsh store. It was later a cinema, the Saxon, until 1983. Emerson College bought it in the mid-1980s, restored it to its first look and reopened it in 1987; it was made a Boston Landmark in 1986.',
  'The ornate front on Tremont Street; the restored auditorium is seen by going to a performance (opera, concerts and Emerson College shows). Part of the Piano Row historic district.',
  accn='Emerson College, 219 Tremont Street. Open for performances; box-office hours were not checked.',
  links=[W('Cutler Majestic Theatre')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('orpheum-theatre-boston','Orpheum Theatre (old Boston Music Hall)','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.35622,-71.06083,'site','open','walk','','1852; rebuilt 1915–1916',
  'Built in 1852 as the Boston Music Hall, designed by Snell and Gregerson, it is one of the oldest theatres in the country. Its great German-built organ was taken out and moved to the Methuen Memorial Music Hall, finished by 1909. Loew\'s bought it in 1915 and reopened it in 1916 with a wholly new interior; it became a rock concert hall in 1971 and was renovated again in 2009.',
  'A concert theatre entered from Hamilton Place, the old alley entrance; the auditorium is the 1916 interior, seen by going to a show.',
  accn='A working concert theatre, 1 Hamilton Place. Open for shows; box-office hours were not checked.',
  links=[W('Orpheum Theatre (Boston)')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('jordan-hall-boston','Jordan Hall, New England Conservatory','Boston','bos','civic','standing',42.34111,-71.08639,'exact','open','walk','','1903',
  'The 1,051-seat concert hall of the New England Conservatory, opened in 1903 as a gift of Eben D. Jordan and dedicated on October 20, 1903 by the Boston Symphony Orchestra. Its square plan follows the lot, and it is known for its acoustics. The conservatory building that holds it is a National Historic Landmark; the hall was restored in 1995.',
  'The conservatory building on Huntington Avenue; the hall with its horseshoe balcony and organ is seen by going to a concert.',
  accn='New England Conservatory, 290 Huntington Avenue. Open for concerts; the conservatory\'s page refused to open, so hours were not checked.',
  links=[W('Jordan Hall (Boston)')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fort-independence-castle-island','Fort Independence, Castle Island','Boston','bos','fort','standing',42.33806,-71.01167,'exact','public','walk','forts','1833–1851',
  'A granite bastion fort on Castle Island in South Boston, guarding the harbor on a site fortified since the 1630s; the English Castle William was rebuilt here from 1692 and renamed after the Revolution. The present fort was built between 1833 and 1851. A small part of Castle William\'s brick survives, covered by the later stonework.',
  'The granite walls of the fort on Castle Island, in the state park. The inside of the fort is open only on tour days.',
  accn='A state park run by the Massachusetts Department of Conservation and Recreation, with the Castle Island Association, in South Boston. The state\'s page and the 2026 tour sheet would not open here: search-result text quoting them gives free fort tours Saturday and Sunday noon to 3 p.m. in July and August 2026, with summer Thursday twilight viewings, and says the fort opens Memorial Day to Columbus Day with varying hours (not confirmed). OpenStreetMap maps a parking lot about 200 m west of the fort, inside the park.',
  links=[W('Fort Independence (Massachusetts)'),['DCR: Castle Island','https://www.mass.gov/locations/castle-island-pleasure-bay-m-street-beach-and-carson-beach']],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.33863,-71.01409],thn='Castle Island parking lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Fort tours Saturday and Sunday noon to 3 p.m. in July and August (2026 schedule, as quoted; not confirmed)'),
N('hotel-alexandra-boston','Hotel Alexandra','Boston','bos','hotel','standing',42.3366,-71.0775,'exact','view','walk','','1875',
  'A High Victorian Gothic hotel with a sandstone front, opened in 1875 at Washington Street and Massachusetts Avenue by the Walworth brothers of the Walworth Manufacturing Company, to designs by Peabody and Stearns; it had a rare steam-powered elevator. It declined after the Washington Street elevated railway was built outside in the early 1900s and was mostly empty by the 1990s. Plans since 2018 to keep the front and build behind it have come and gone; a new plan was filed in April 2025.',
  'The ornate five-storey sandstone front, vacant. Part of the South End Landmark District.',
  accn='Private and vacant, 1759–1763 Washington Street. Seen from Washington Street and Massachusetts Avenue only. The Silver Line stops nearby.',
  links=[W('Hotel Alexandra (Boston)')],brick='?'),
]
